Christian atheism embraces the teachings, narratives, symbols, practices, or communities associated with Christianity without accepting the existence of a deity. It is a form of nontheism and often overlaps with post-theism.
There are different schools of thought among Christian atheists. Thomas Ogletree, Frederick Marquand Professor of Ethics and Religious Studies at Yale Divinity School, lists these four common beliefs:
the assertion of the unreality of God for our age, including the understandings of God that have been a part of traditional Christian theology;
the insistence upon coming to grips with contemporary culture as a necessary feature of responsible theological work;
varying degrees and forms of alienation from the church as it is now constituted, and;
recognition of the centrality of the person of Jesus in theological reflection.
One approach to Christian atheism is Death of God theology, which had brief public prominence in the mid-1960s.
